<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Talaria asks a few applications for the document you are working in, in the
    only language they answer: Word draws its own text and exposes nothing to
    the accessibility tree, so it is asked over Apple Events instead.

    Required because the bundle is signed with the hardened runtime. Without
    this key an Apple Event is refused as errAEEventNotPermitted and — the part
    that costs the afternoon — macOS shows no permission prompt at all, because
    the process is not entitled to ask for one. The usage string in Info.plist
    is what the prompt says; this is what allows there to be a prompt.

    Still gated by the user: the first event to each application raises the
    ordinary Automation prompt naming that application, and is refused until
    granted. This entitlement buys the right to ask, not the right to proceed.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
